          "const ENCODINGS = {\n  br: \".br\",\n  gzip: \".gz\",\n  zstd: \".zst\",\n} as const;\nconst ENCODINGS_ORDERED_KEYS: (keyof typeof ENCODINGS)[] = [\n  \"br\",\n  \"zstd\",\n  \"gzip\",\n];"
        ],
        "finalMessage": "I fixed issue 1 in `index.ts:143`. I couldn't re-run the linter because the shell command needed approval, which this run can't get, so the fix hasn't been checked by a lint pass.\n\n**What I changed:**\n- **The object keys are now sorted:** `br`, `gzip`, `zstd`.\n- **The preference order is now written out:** `ENCODINGS_ORDERED_KEYS` used to be built from `Object.keys(ENCODINGS)`. It's now an explicit `[\"br\", \"zstd\", \"gzip\"]` with the same type.\n\nSorting the keys alone would have changed how the code behaves. The `ENCODINGS_ORDERED_KEYS` loop at line 241 serves the first compressed file the client accepts, so it took its preference order from the object's key order. After sorting, a client accepting both zstd and gzip would have been sent gzip instead of zstd. With the order listed separately, the object can stay sorted and the server still prefers br, then zstd, then gzip.",
